Java I/O Streams are used to handle data flow in Java.
Data flows like:
Source → Program → Destination

There are two types:
Input Stream → Reads data
Output Stream → Writes data

1. Byte Streams (Binary Data)

Used for:

Images
Videos
Audio
Binary files

Base Classes:
InputStream
OutputStream

Common Classes:
FileInputStream
FileOutputStream
BufferedInputStream
BufferedOutputStream

2. Character Streams (Text Data)

Used for:

Text files
Strings

Base Classes:
Reader
Writer

Common Classes:
FileReader
FileWriter
BufferedReader
BufferedWriter

3. Byte Stream Example

Used to copy binary files.

Important Methods
read() → reads one byte
write() → writes one byte
close() → closes stream

4. Character Stream Example

Used for reading and writing text files.

Example Classes
FileReader
FileWriter

Reads characters one by one.

5. Buffered Streams

Used for faster performance.

Advantages
Faster I/O
Efficient memory usage
Reads line by line using readLine()

6. Important Methods
| Method       | Purpose        |
| ------------ | -------------- |
| `read()`     | Read data      |
| `write()`    | Write data     |
| `close()`    | Close stream   |
| `flush()`    | Clear buffer   |
| `readLine()` | Read full line |

7. Byte Stream vs Character Stream
| Byte Stream               | Character Stream     |
| ------------------------- | -------------------- |
| Used for binary data      | Used for text data   |
| Base class: `InputStream` | Base class: `Reader` |
| Example: Images           | Example: Text files  |
